.TH FGETS 3S  "May 15, 1985"
.AT 3
.SH NAME
fgets \- get a string from a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.B char *fgets(s, n, stream)
.br
.B char *s;
.br
.B int n;
.br
.SM
.B FILE
.B *stream;
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Fgets
reads 
.IR n \-1
characters, or up through a newline
character, whichever comes first,
from the
.I stream
into the string
.IR s .
The last character read into
.I s
is followed by a null character.
.I Fgets
returns its first argument.
.PP
The newline is kept, so a caller that wants a bare line removes it,
usually with
.IR index (3).
A line longer than
.IR n \-1
characters arrives as two calls; the caller decides whether to join
them, to treat the tail as its own line, or to read to the next
newline and discard it.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
puts(3S),
getc(3S),
scanf(3S),
fread(3S),
ferror(3S)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Fgets
returns the constant pointer 
.SM
.B NULL
upon end of file or error.
.SH NOTES
.IR Gets (3S)
is not provided.
It takes no length, so every call stores as many characters as the line
holds, and the process image is a single window of text, data, bss and
stack with no guard page between them.
.B <stdio.h>
does not declare it and the library carries no definition, so a call to
it fails to compile.
